## 1. Introduction

The question of what makes a governing class legitimate has occupied political philosophy since Plato. Contemporary answers typically invoke consent of the governed (Locke), democratic mandate (Rousseau, Mill), rational-legal authority (Weber), or performance outcomes (contemporary public administration).

This paper proposes an additional and largely overlooked determinant: whether the governing class *as a class* was tested by conflict before it assumed power. We argue that pre-governing conflict — war, revolution, sustained ideological struggle, or existential resistance — functions as a necessary condition for the long-term legitimacy and stability of the resulting state.

The claim is testable. It predicts that states whose governing classes acquired power without prior conflict will trend toward political dysfunction over time, ranging from mild institutional weakness to full failed-state conditions, in proportion to the degree of conflict deficiency and inversely to the strength of inherited institutional memory.

